now sakia starmer described a day of  shame for labour as he accepted all the  findings of an investigation into its  failure to deal with anti-semitism but  now he's facing more pressure from  within the party  following the suspension of his  predecessor jeremy corbyn  he also had the whip withdrawn for his  response to the report's  findings now there's talk of a civil war  emerging within labour well let's talk  to sakiya stamina now  very good morning to you first and  foremost on the back of what jeremy  corbyn said yesterday is there  yet another need for the labour party to  apologize to the  jewish community well i apologized  uh yesterday in clear terms  i also apologized on the first day i  took over as leader of the  labour party for our failure to deal  with anti-semitism  the findings in the commission's report  yesterday were  clear and rigorous there were findings  of  unlawful action and the right thing to  do in my view is to accept in full  the findings of the commission to  apologize and recognize  the hurt and grief that it has caused  jewish people  in the united kingdom um and to be  absolutely committed to carrying out the  recommendations  in a short period of time and that's  what was my response and  i had hoped yesterday would be if you  like a line in the  sand i'd spoken to many jewish  communities jewish community leaders  that's what they wanted they wanted the  labour party to recognize the hurt and  pain  accept the findings and to move on  um to rebuild trust with the jewish  community that is my  absolute commitment i had hoped that  that's how yesterday would have gone  unfortunately because of the comments of  jeremy corbyn in response to the report  the day's events took a different turn  do you think now in the light of that  that jeremy corbyn is  an anti-semite or at least has said  things  yesterday which were anti-semitic well  the  report was a full report and it made no  individual findings  against jeremy corbyn it's very  important that i say that and make that  absolutely  um clear i was disappointed in jeremy's  response  yesterday particularly since i had said  in my response  that the labour party will not tolerate  anti-semitism  nor will it tolerate those who deny it  as a problem of anti-semitism and say  it's all exaggerated or  factional because that for me is part of  the problem  so i was disappointed in jeremy's  response it is now subject to  an investigation and the one thing i've  learned from the report yesterday is  that  the leader of the labour party should  now not comment  um any further let the process take its  course  one of the findings in the report  yesterday was that there had been um  interference from the  labour leaders office in individual  cases that shouldn't happen  and i'm not going to make that mistake  but but to go back to the point do you  think his  his his claims of over exaggeration of  the anti-semitic  issue for political gain as he put it  do you think that comment by him was in  itself anti-semitic  well you're inviting me to comment on  the very issue that's being investigated  but i can tell you in general terms how  i saw this or i see this  because i made that clear um yesterday  and that is  that if you're anti-semitic then you  shouldn't be in the labour party  if you are in denial about um  anti-semitism in the labour party and  say it's all whipped up it's exaggerated  it's just  factional fighting then that is part of  the problem  too i said it yesterday i've actually  said it before  because that only adds to the grief and  pain  of those that have already been affected  by the failure to deal with  anti-semitism  over the last few years which is why i  was disappointed  with the response of jeremy corbyn  yesterday morning okay  well you've been very clear on that so  where does that leave people like  len mccluskey who obviously is is head  of  your biggest donor the united union i  mean he's he's previously  previously said there was no  anti-semitism  problem within the labour party he is  obviously back jeremy corbyn from what  happened yesterday  can you still take his money well um  what led mccluskey is concerned about is  that there shouldn't be  a split in the labour party um  and he's right about that i don't want  to split in the labour party  i stood um as leader of labour party on  the basis that i would  unite the party but also that i would  tackle anti-semitism i think both of  those can be done  there's no reason for a civil war in our  party  but we are absolutely determined i'm  absolutely determined  to root out anti-semitism i don't want  the words  labor and labour party and anti-semitism  in the same sentence again  that's about rebuilding trust that's my  job  that's the job of the labor leadership  now i know that  the jewish communities are looking at me  and they're saying very clearly we will  judge you by what you do  not by what you say and they're right  about that but i'm determined to restore  that  trust and we can have a united labor  party around  the forgive me for it forgive me for  interrupting forgive me for interrupting  but are you  on a personal level then in the light of  all you've said  this morning are you comfortable with  what len mccluskey said yesterday  well len is um i spoke to len uh  yesterday he is concerned um but there  shouldn't be a civil war in the labour  party  as am i and there's no need for that and  i think that  it's the responsibility of all of us to  work forward in a way that  unites our party but i will not renege  on my commitment  to root out anti-semitism in the labour  party  but he has said there was he has  previously said there wasn't an issue  with anti-semitism within the labor  party  well and i've previously said that  denying this a problem is part of the  problem but so can you send  his money len mccluskey and i do not  want to split in the labour party nobody  in the labour party wants splits or  arguments  we need to be united in the labour party  but we must  not pull back from the commitment to  root out anti-semitism  you will see though sakir what you how  people watching this will say  you know you you you're prepared or the  party is prepared to take a very strong  line  against individuals but but not wanting  to take a particularly strong line  with the head of the union which is  providing you with millions of pounds  look i mean i've been leader for six  months i've already brought about change  and i've made some very difficult  decisions  uh that frankly people didn't think i  would make  so i don't really think that the  suggestion that  somehow i'm not strong on anti-semitism  and i'm not going to deal with this  is a fair reflection of the last six  months  wherever issues uh are um raised  they are now dealt with properly by the  complaints process  and they will be  again can i just get a clear answer on  the money side you're happy are you  to keep taking money from unite whose  leader  has said there isn't a problem with  anti-semitism within the labour party  well uh len mccluskey wasn't saying  yesterday that there isn't a problem  with anti-semitism he said that  previously when he said it previously i  said that  saying this whole thing is part of the  problem but he didn't say that yesterday  he recognizes  what the problem is i spoke to len  mccluskey yesterday he recognizes  the anti-semitism in the labour party he  doesn't want to split  in the labour party i think we can have  a united party  but we must also tackle anti-semitism  um well the the report was was very  clear  wasn't it and you said you've accepted  it in terms of how this was  dealt with uh prior to your appointment  as leader  and the reflection on that is that it  was that it was not handled well that  and as you said as jeremy corbyn  appeared to intervene in some cases  that should have been been handled by  the the party executive  in the light of that you still  campaigned for him to be  uh our prime minister at the end of last  year  why did you do that why did you stand  with him why didn't you step down from  the shadow cabinet  well jeremy corbyn was twice elected  leader of the labour party he had a  clear mandate  i served in the shadow cabinet as the  shadow brexit secretary dealing with the  brexit negotiations within the shadow  cabinet and  actually outside on media including i  think on sky  i spoke out about anti-semitism i  thought it was right to raise it  inside the shadow cabinet and outside  the shadow cabinet  but there's no getting away from the  findings of the report yesterday  they are clear findings and we all have  to accept them including myself  which is why i thought the right  response yesterday was to accept the  findings to apologize again  for the hurt that had been caused and to  make it my absolute business  to implement the recommendations as  quickly as possible and that's why  we've already agreed an action plan will  be put before the commission within six  weeks  setting out what action practical steps  we will take  on the back of the report you're a very  clever man you must have had some  inkling as to what was going on while  you were serving in the shadow cabinet  i mean did you let yourself down by  staying in position  i think there was a lot of challenge  within the shutter cabinet believe you  me  on issues of the role changes on the  international  definition where there should be  automatic expulsion of those that have  been  anti-semitic i was very clear in those  discussions i was very clear outside of  the shadow cabinet  on the media that's a matter of record i  thought we needed voices in and outside  the shadow cabinet  but the findings yesterday our findings  um  in relation to the labour party to the  collective leadership  we all need to accept them including me  um and that is why i apologized  yesterday  it's also why i apologize is the first  thing i did as labour leader on the 4th  of  april when i set out my apology in my  acceptance speech  and set out my commitment that as the  labour leader it was my  duty to root out anti-semitism and i'm  not going to shirk from that duty  what did you make of your deputy's  comments yesterday when when she said  jeremy corbyn has a blind spot  when it comes to this issue well angela  rainer knows  uh jeremy very well um and um  i was disappointed as she was in  jeremiah's response  yesterday i don't want to get too far  into the criticism of the response  because that is a matter of the  due process now but but andrew and i  spoke extensively yesterday we were both  really disappointed with the response  this was a day  i had hoped where the labour party could  draw a line a painful  day of course in light of the findings  but  draw a line and move forward and i know  that's what  jewish communities wanted they wanted us  to accept the report  and to move forward on the  recommendations that is how i had hoped  yesterday  would play out um it didn't  and that's why angela rayner said what  she did it's why she and i were very  disappointed and obviously she and i  uh worked very closely yesterday as we  always do it always  it's almost that i mean you can  understand k i mean come on  from your your previous career you can  understand how that almost sounds sort  of slightly apologetic  well of course i mean i'm i am  apologetic i've apologized  forgive me forgive me you've you've  misunderstood sort of  apologetic towards jeremy corbyn or it's  just a bit of a blind spot  oh no look i i don't think answers being  apologetic in any  shape or form look the decision that was  taken yesterday  was the right decision but it was a  difficult decision  just as there have been other difficult  decisions in the last six months  and the one thing angela rainer and i  have been absolutely clear about as  as as the leader and deputy leader of  the parties we are not going to shy away  from difficult decisions  yesterday was a very difficult decision  for the labour party  it was the right decision we now need to  let the investigation take its course  but i don't think  in light of the decision was taken  yesterday anybody could say we're being  apologetic on the contrary i think many  people are saying  i didn't think that the labour party  would take such a difficult  decision we did because of our  commitment to rooting out anti-semitism  you've talked about not wanting a split  in the labour party you've talked about  len mccluskey not wanting a split in the  labour party  if it is necessary if a split is  actually necessary  to to save the labour party from these  calls of anti-semitism and  some some form of the the sort of racist  undertones of all of that would you be  prepared to split the party  i don't think it's necessary and my job  is to  unite the party i don't think it's  necessary and i don't think  it will happen we need to work together  the vast majority  of labour party members and supporters  are horrified by what's going on in  relation to anti-semitism  and the findings in the report yesterday  they want to pull together  they want to see united party and we  will work to that end  but it has to sit alongside the  commitment  to root out anti-semitism because that  is a matter of values  and trust and confidence that goes way  beyond  the question of simply winning an  election or not it goes to the heart of  what the labour party is  in in terms of of the heart of what the  labour party is i mean  you'll have seen in the papers this  morning there is there is lots of talk  as to whether  you are having a purge of the militant  faction as  as neil kinnick did back in the 80s  are you trying to to drag the labour  party back to its heart as you see it  i'm not purging um anybody or any group  within  the labour party what i'm being very  clear about is that the labour party  that i  lead will not tolerate anti-semitism  full stop nor will it tolerate those  that say  anti-semitism in the labour party  doesn't really exist it's exaggerated  or it's just a factional war whipped up  in or outside the labour party including  by  the media so i'm being very clear  about that but i'm not in the business  of purging anyone  from the labour party i'm in the  business of taking difficult decisions  to follow through on a commitment that i  made not just to our jewish communities  but to the british public  but as leader of the labour party i  would tackle anti-semitism  and i'm absolutely determined to do that  sakia stalmer we appreciate your time  today thank you  thank you

